Summary
I've successfully resolved the C/Fortran linking issue using ISO_C_BINDING with proper architecture. Here's the clean, compiler-based solution (no binary manipulation):

Problem
The fnb demo couldn't link because:

C code provides func, stpnt, bcnd, icnd, pvls, fopt (plain C names without underscore)
Fortran by default appends trailing underscore to external symbols (func_, stpnt_, etc.)
AUTO library (compiled with default settings) calls func_, stpnt_, etc. but couldn't find them from the C code
Solution Architecture
fhn_noheader.c - C implementation without #include "auto_f2c.h" header

Avoids the static declarations in auto_f2c.h that made symbols file-local
Functions exported as globally-visible C symbols
fhn_wrapper.f90 - Fortran wrapper using ISO_C_BINDING

Wrapper subroutines func, stpnt, etc. with bind(C, name='func_'), bind(C, name='stpnt_'), etc.
These export as Fortran-mangled names (func_, stpnt_, etc.) for AUTO library compatibility
Internal interface declarations call C functions with bind(C, name='func'), etc.
CMakeLists.txt - Clean build configuration

No binary manipulation tools (objcopy)
Uses standard ISO_C_BINDING for C/Fortran interoperability
References proper global C symbols
Result
✅ Clean executable links successfully
✅ Uses ISO_C_BINDING standard (F2003 Fortran standard)
✅ No objcopy workarounds or compiler flag surprises
✅ Proper symbol resolution (both C and Fortran names available)
✅ Standards-compliant solution